1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,817
Ciao. This is Liam, in for Bear, with a short film for INFO 7375, Computational

2
00:00:04,837 --> 00:00:09,654
Skepticism for AI. You might ask what kind of person should supervise an AI. The

3
00:00:09,674 --> 00:00:13,846
chapter says that is the wrong question. Not personality traits, not vibes. The

4
00:00:13,866 --> 00:00:18,360
question is what a supervisor can do that the system cannot do for itself.

5
00:00:18,430 --> 00:00:22,915
Four terms. Capacity: something the supervisor can do that the system cannot do for

6
00:00:22,935 --> 00:00:28,064
itself. Prior: the sense, built over years, of what kinds of cases tend to be what

7
00:00:28,084 --> 00:00:32,569
kinds of cases. Formulation: the exact question the system is being asked to answer.

8
00:00:32,589 --> 00:00:36,430
And integration: many outputs made into one decision that someone stands behind.

9
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:41,172
Verification is the problem. A model makes an output, and someone has to check it

10
00:00:41,192 --> 00:00:45,239
against the world. That someone is the supervisor, standing between the model and

11
00:00:45,259 --> 00:00:49,619
the world. The chapter counts five things the supervisor does there that the model

12
00:00:49,639 --> 00:00:50,870
cannot do for itself.

13
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:54,717
One. Plausibility auditing. The chapter's case: a low triage score for a

14
00:00:54,737 --> 00:00:58,514
forty-nine-year-old with a swollen leg. An experienced clinician looks at it, and

15
00:00:58,534 --> 00:01:02,943
something twitches. The twitch is the audit: a check against a prior, the sense

16
00:01:02,963 --> 00:01:06,740
built over years of what kinds of cases tend to be what.

17
00:01:06,810 --> 00:01:11,276
The model cannot do this for itself. It has its training data. The supervisor has

18
00:01:11,296 --> 00:01:15,763
the world. When nobody audits, the failure is quiet: the score arrives in a credible

19
00:01:15,783 --> 00:01:19,950
format, nobody asks whether it makes sense on its face, and it goes through.

20
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:24,533
Two. Problem formulation. The chapter says most AI failures are failures of

21
00:01:24,553 --> 00:01:28,311
formulation, not performance. The triage system answered: what statistical category

22
00:01:28,331 --> 00:01:32,844
is this presentation? The clinical question was: how likely is a life-threatening

23
00:01:32,864 --> 00:01:37,000
condition? The first was answered well. The second was never asked.

24
00:01:37,070 --> 00:01:41,156
Three. Tool orchestration. AI is one tool among many. For each sub-problem, the

25
00:01:41,176 --> 00:01:45,261
supervisor picks the tool: the AI, human judgment, a different model, or, the

26
00:01:45,281 --> 00:01:50,630
hardest call, no tool yet: we do not know how to do this well enough to deploy.

27
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:54,954
Saying we should not deploy this here is supervisory work, and often the most

28
00:01:54,974 --> 00:01:59,229
valuable work the supervisor does. The failure runs the other way: AI used because

29
00:01:59,249 --> 00:02:03,809
it is available, not because it is right for the task, because nobody has the

30
00:02:03,829 --> 00:02:05,030
mandate to say no.

31
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:09,082
Four. Interpretive judgment. The chapter's example: a 5% false-positive rate is fine

32
00:02:09,102 --> 00:02:13,750
for a spam filter, and catastrophic for a cancer screen. The same number, two

33
00:02:13,770 --> 00:02:18,752
meanings. The number is not the meaning; the meaning is the number in the domain.

34
00:02:18,772 --> 00:02:22,420
The failure: numbers read off a dashboard with no domain translation.

35
00:02:22,490 --> 00:02:26,850
Five. Executive integration. Outputs from many tools, many models and many people,

36
00:02:26,870 --> 00:02:30,865
synthesized into one decision that someone stands behind. Somewhere in the

37
00:02:30,885 --> 00:02:36,705
organization, the buck has to stop. If there is no such place, the system has no

38
00:02:36,725 --> 00:02:37,800
decision-maker, only outputs.

39
00:02:37,870 --> 00:02:43,228
The chapter calls this the rarest capacity, and the one most often missing from AI

40
00:02:43,248 --> 00:02:48,247
deployments, because no model produces it and most pipelines have no place for it.

41
00:02:48,267 --> 00:02:52,549
The failure: the pipeline produces decisions nobody owns, and when something goes

42
00:02:52,569 --> 00:02:54,700
wrong, accountability dissolves into the architecture.

43
00:02:54,770 --> 00:02:59,270
These five are vocabulary for now. By the end of the book, you can look at any

44
00:02:59,290 --> 00:03:02,992
deployment and name, for each step, which capacity is exercised and by whom. Where

45
00:03:03,012 --> 00:03:07,512
you cannot name it, you have found a gap. And in the chapter's words: gaps are where

46
00:03:07,532 --> 00:03:08,310
the patients die.

47
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:13,485
Your turn. Pick one AI deployment you know, or one step of one, and paste this into

48
00:03:13,505 --> 00:03:18,610
Claude: Here is one AI deployment I know, or one step of it: describe it. For each

49
00:03:18,630 --> 00:03:21,624
of the five supervisory capacities, plausibility auditing, problem formulation, tool

50
00:03:21,644 --> 00:03:24,337
orchestration, interpretive judgment and executive integration, write who exercises

51
00:03:24,357 --> 00:03:29,462
it, in one line each, or write nobody. Then tell me which line you are least sure

52
00:03:29,482 --> 00:03:34,285
of, and why. Then check two things yourself. Is every who a named role, not the

53
00:03:34,305 --> 00:03:37,300
team? And did you write nobody at least once, honestly?

54
00:03:37,370 --> 00:03:42,420
Five Things the Model Can't Do for Itself. At Nik Bear Brown.
